  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "Where is the center of the circumcircle of a right triangle?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "At the vertex of the right angle",
    "B": "At the foot of the altitude to the hypotenuse",
    "C": "At the midpoint of the hypotenuse"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "The chord subtending the right angle is a diameter, so the hypotenuse is the diameter of the circumcircle, and its center is the midpoint of the hypotenuse.\n\nAlternative method 1: Find a point equally far from the vertices\n\nLet \\(\\triangle ABC\\) have its right angle at \\(A\\), and let \\(M\\) be the midpoint of hypotenuse \\(BC\\). The median-to-the-hypotenuse theorem gives\n\\[\nAM=\\frac{BC}{2}=BM=CM.\n\\]\nThus the circle centered at \\(M\\) with radius \\(BC/2\\) passes through all three vertices. It is the circumcircle, so its center is the midpoint of the hypotenuse, choice C.",
